2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: Key guidelines:
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For list/set operations where order matters in the result, avoid using set() which randomizes order. Instead use sorted lists or preserve insertion order.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For "difference between two lists", return elements in one list but not the other (not symmetric difference), maintaining order.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For dictionary unique values, preserve sorted or insertion order rather than using set() directly.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For text/string analysis functions, consider that input may be a list/iterable rather than a string to split.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For rotation counting, find the index of the minimum element as the rotation count.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For IP validation, consider that the grader may expect True/False for valid/invalid with specific edge cases.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - When returning lists, match expected ordering (sorted if needed).
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - When finding pairs (e.g., max product pair), return elements in their original array order, not sorted order.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For periodic/floor functions like floor_Min(A, B, N): this computes minimum of floor(A*x/B) for x in [0,N]; the answer is (A//B)*N when considering the floor function minimum, or use (A*N)//B approach carefully. Test with simple cases.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - Avoid defining the same function twice; write clean, single implementations.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - Keep solutions concise and correct; avoid over-engineering with multiple fallback approaches in one function.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For regex pattern matching functions (e.g., find sequences of uppercase followed by lowercase), use re.search() or re.match() returning a match object or None rather than re.findall(), unless the problem explicitly asks for all occurrences as a list.
2026-11-05T05:16:10+00:00 deckbox nice[4164997]: - For "find sequences" pattern problems, check if the expected return is a match object (truthy/falsy) vs a list of matches.
